  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure passes through this evening, and quickly moves \noff to the east through the overnight hours. Meanwhile, low \npressure tracks eastward through the Great Lakes overnight \ntonight, with rain pushing into western New Hampshire by sunrise\ntomorrow.\n\nPeriods of rain and showers move through during the morning, \nand become more showery by the afternoon across much of New \nHampshire and western Maine. Low pressure tracks near the \nCanadian border through the day, with a cold front crossing \nthrough late in the day and through the evening hours. Some \nadditional pop up showers or even a few thunderstorms will be \npossible across southern New Hampshire where a few peaks of sun \nhelp temps push into the mid 60s during the day. Overall around \na half inch of rainfall is expected, with a little less across \nsouthern areas, and a little more to the north.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe low pressure system deepens across the Canadian Maritimes \non Saturday, while high pressure is building westward into New \nEngland. This sets up a gusty northwesterly wind, and transports\na Canadian airmass that's cold for mid October. Wind gusts of \n25-35 mph are expected through the daytime, while highs range \nfrom the 30s across the north, to only near 50 along the coast.\n\nAcross the higher terrain, wintertime conditions are expected \nfrom Friday night through Sunday morning. Accumulating snowfall \nacross the upslope terrain, temperatures in the teens, and wind \nchills well below zero are all expected. Wind gusts over 100 mph\nare expected on Mount Washington on Saturday. Any hikers or \nthose above 2500ft should plan for wintertime conditions this \nweekend.\n\nScattered snow showers across the mountains on Saturday morning\ngradually decrease in coverage through the day, but a few \nflurries are likely to drift downwind of the mountains through \nthe day while gradually drying up.\n\nSaturday night will then by far be the coldest night of the \nseason across the area. Widespread lows in the 20s and breezy \nconditions are in store for Saturday night. Lows below freezing \nare expected to the coastline across the region as well.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure crests over the region on Sunday and looks to \nstay nosed in through at least the daylight hours of next \nThursday. This will bring an extended period of mild daytime \ntemperatures, and likely some pretty chilly nights due to \nradiational cooling, as well as dry weather.\n\nSunday will be the transition day with high temperatures in the\nlow to mid-50s with mostly clear skies, but after a raw \nSaturday this will probably feel comfortable. Afterwards, most \ndays should feature abundant sunshine with high temperatures in \nthe upper 50s and 60s. Periods of clouds are possible with \nsubtle troughing aloft, but the midlevel airmass looks very dry \nwhich will preclude any threat for precipitation. Low \ntemperatures look to be generally around normal being in the \nupper 20s to mid-30s north to south, but of course any night \nwhere strong radiational cooling takes place, can end up quite \ncold. Looking at long range ensembles 850-700 mb temperatures \nstay right around the 0 C mark through this stretch so certainly\nsome cold nights could come to fruition, but nothing crazy.",
